To upgrade your implementation from a hover effect to a popup, the approach you have started is on the right track, but it may need some adjustments to ensure the popup functions correctly. Here’s how to fix the problem based on the code you provided.

1. **HTML Structure**: Your HTML structure is set up to show the message in a popup. Ensure that the `span` containing the popup message has the appropriate styles to hide it initially. You might want to add a class that controls its visibility (`show` in your case) using CSS.

2. **CSS for Popup**: You should define the CSS styles for the `.popup-thumb-span` class to manage the visibility. For example, set it to `display: none;` initially and configure it to `display: block;` when the class `show` is applied.

   ```css
   .popup-thumb-span {
       display: none; /* Hide initially */
       position: absolute; /* Positioning the popup */
       background-color: #fff; /* Background color */
       border: 1px solid #ccc; /* Optional border */
       padding: 10px; /* Padding for the text */
       z-index: 1000; /* Ensures it appears above other elements */
   }

   .popup-thumb-span.show {
       display: block; /* Show the popup */
   }
   ```

3. **JavaScript Functionality**: The JavaScript function you've written correctly toggles the visibility of the popup. Ensure it is correctly referenced in your HTML.

   ```html
   <div class="happy-thumb popup" onclick="myFunction()">
       <p>happy
           <span class="popup-thumb-span" id="happyPopup">I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made. Wonderful are your works; my soul knows it very well. Psalm 139:14</span>
       </p>
   </div>
   ```

4. **Position of the Popup**: If you want the popup to appear near the clicked element, you might want to adjust its position using JavaScript. Here’s an example of how to calculate its position:

   ```javascript
   function myFunction() {
       var popup = document.getElementById("happyPopup");
       popup.classList.toggle("show");

       // Positioning the popup below the clicked element (optional)
       var rect = event.currentTarget.getBoundingClientRect();
       popup.style.top = (rect.bottom + window.scrollY) + 'px';
       popup.style.left = (rect.left + window.scrollX) + 'px';
   }
   ```

5. **Closing the Popup**: Consider adding functionality that allows the popup to close when clicked elsewhere. You can do this by adding an event listener to the document:

   ```javascript
   document.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
       let isClickInside = document.querySelector('.happy-thumb').contains(event.target);
       if (!isClickInside) {
           var popup = document.getElementById("happyPopup");
           popup.classList.remove("show");
       }
   });
   ```

This setup will achieve your objective of displaying a scripture associated with the emotion selected by clicking on the respective element, transitioning from a hover action to a click action. Make sure to test the code thoroughly within your provided CodePen link to verify that the popups display as expected [SO-9750310].

